1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,160
Microsoft 365.

2
00:00:02,120 --> 00:00:04,580
Copilot agents are reusable mini assistants

3
00:00:04,580 --> 00:00:07,300
you can configure for a specific workflow.

4
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:09,620
Instead of re-explaining the same task

5
00:00:09,620 --> 00:00:11,140
every time you open a new chat,

6
00:00:11,540 --> 00:00:13,380
you can give the agent a clear role,

7
00:00:13,720 --> 00:00:15,980
standing instructions, approved knowledge sources,

8
00:00:16,300 --> 00:00:17,420
and optional capabilities.

9
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:19,840
Think of it as turning a super prompt

10
00:00:19,840 --> 00:00:21,160
into a reusable assistant.

11
00:00:21,740 --> 00:00:25,320
You can create agents from the Microsoft 365 Copilot app,

12
00:00:25,640 --> 00:00:27,100
OneDrive or SharePoint,

13
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:28,920
depending on your license, permissions,

14
00:00:28,920 --> 00:00:30,520
and organization settings.

15
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,320
For this demo, I'll use the most common path,

16
00:00:33,800 --> 00:00:36,240
the Microsoft 365 Copilot app.

17
00:00:36,660 --> 00:00:38,660
To begin, I go to the left side panel

18
00:00:38,660 --> 00:00:39,640
and click New Agent.

19
00:00:39,960 --> 00:00:41,860
Here, I can either describe the agent

20
00:00:41,860 --> 00:00:43,560
I want to create in the prompt box,

21
00:00:43,880 --> 00:00:45,600
or skip directly to configuration.

22
00:00:46,440 --> 00:00:47,980
Since I already know what I want to build,

23
00:00:48,220 --> 00:00:49,160
I'll skip to configuration.

24
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:51,800
In this example, I'll build an assistant

25
00:00:51,800 --> 00:00:54,160
that helps me write weekly leadership updates.

26
00:00:54,720 --> 00:00:55,620
I'll name it Larry.

27
00:00:56,140 --> 00:00:57,740
Then I add a short description.

28
00:00:58,360 --> 00:01:00,440
Helps me write weekly leadership updates.

29
00:01:01,260 --> 00:01:02,800
Next, I configure the agent.

30
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:05,620
This brings me to the most important part,

31
00:01:06,320 --> 00:01:07,300
the instructions field.

32
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,460
This is where I paste my tested prompt template,

33
00:01:10,800 --> 00:01:12,200
personalized to my workflow.

34
00:01:12,940 --> 00:01:15,000
For this example, the instructions include

35
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:16,580
four consistent elements,

36
00:01:16,960 --> 00:01:19,300
aligned with CEO do super prompting,

37
00:01:19,840 --> 00:01:20,860
the role of the assistant,

38
00:01:21,340 --> 00:01:22,520
the objective of the workflow,

39
00:01:22,860 --> 00:01:23,960
the do's and don'ts,

40
00:01:23,960 --> 00:01:26,980
and the output format I want every time.

41
00:01:27,480 --> 00:01:29,480
The fifth element is the variable part.

42
00:01:30,060 --> 00:01:32,280
That is what I provide each time I use the assistant,

43
00:01:32,460 --> 00:01:33,560
such as this week's notes,

44
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:36,460
priorities, metrics, or meeting context.

45
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:40,400
The goal is not to write a long prompt every time.

46
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:42,140
The goal is to write it once,

47
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:45,060
test it, improve it, and make it reusable.

48
00:01:46,020 --> 00:01:48,140
Then I can choose the knowledge sources.

49
00:01:48,700 --> 00:01:51,040
Agent Builder can use sources like SharePoint,

50
00:01:51,420 --> 00:01:54,640
OneDrive, websites, teams, and approved connectors,

51
00:01:54,640 --> 00:01:56,760
depending on your organization's settings.

52
00:01:57,420 --> 00:01:58,680
The practical rule is simple.

53
00:01:59,040 --> 00:02:00,860
Only add sources that improve the workflow.

54
00:02:01,360 --> 00:02:03,260
Too much context can create noise,

55
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:05,740
contradictions, or outdated references.

56
00:02:06,280 --> 00:02:07,940
For this leadership update assistant,

57
00:02:08,140 --> 00:02:09,660
I'll keep it prompt based first.

58
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:12,340
After that, I review the agent's capabilities.

59
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:13,920
Depending on your setup,

60
00:02:14,240 --> 00:02:16,820
you may be able to enable features like document creation,

61
00:02:17,140 --> 00:02:18,920
charts, code, or images.

62
00:02:19,460 --> 00:02:22,100
For this assistant, I'll enable document creation

63
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,520
so the final output can be turned

64
00:02:24,520 --> 00:02:26,580
into a Word document more easily.

65
00:02:27,160 --> 00:02:28,700
I can also add starter prompts.

66
00:02:29,340 --> 00:02:30,900
This is optional, but useful

67
00:02:30,900 --> 00:02:32,920
if you want to share the agent to others.

68
00:02:33,520 --> 00:02:34,940
For Larry, I might add,

69
00:02:35,420 --> 00:02:38,020
draft this week's leadership update for my notes.

70
00:02:38,720 --> 00:02:40,100
Turn these project updates

71
00:02:40,100 --> 00:02:42,220
into an executive-ready summary.

72
00:02:42,920 --> 00:02:45,840
Rewrite this update to make it clearer and more concise.

73
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:48,560
These prompts help people understand

74
00:02:48,560 --> 00:02:50,020
what the agent is for

75
00:02:50,020 --> 00:02:51,880
without needing a separate explanation.

76
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:53,680
And before I finalize it,

77
00:02:53,880 --> 00:02:55,820
I choose Preview to test the agent.

78
00:02:56,200 --> 00:02:58,360
I paste the variable part of my workflow.

79
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,380
Here are my inputs for the upcoming leadership meeting.

80
00:03:01,700 --> 00:03:02,720
Please process them.

81
00:03:03,180 --> 00:03:04,480
Then I add my notes.

82
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:07,440
The agent generates a draft leadership update,

83
00:03:07,720 --> 00:03:09,040
and now I review it.

84
00:03:09,400 --> 00:03:09,980
Is it clear?

85
00:03:10,380 --> 00:03:11,580
Is it executive-ready?

86
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:13,980
Does it follow the structure I asked for?

87
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:16,880
Does it avoid details that are too tactical?

88
00:03:16,880 --> 00:03:20,500
If not, I can refine the instructions and test again.

89
00:03:21,100 --> 00:03:22,820
Since I'm satisfied with the results,

90
00:03:23,200 --> 00:03:24,300
I click on Create.

91
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:27,220
By default, the agent starts as private,

92
00:03:27,540 --> 00:03:29,840
but sharing options may include your organization

93
00:03:29,840 --> 00:03:32,800
or specific users, depending on admin policy.

94
00:03:33,380 --> 00:03:35,620
Once created, the agent usually appears

95
00:03:35,620 --> 00:03:39,180
in the left-side panel in Microsoft 365 Copilot.

96
00:03:39,580 --> 00:03:41,980
In some Microsoft 365 experiences,

97
00:03:42,540 --> 00:03:43,860
I can also call an agent

98
00:03:43,860 --> 00:03:45,520
by ad mentioning it in a new chat.

99
00:03:45,960 --> 00:03:48,620
That lets me bring the agent into the flow of work

100
00:03:48,620 --> 00:03:50,220
instead of switching contexts.

101
00:03:50,960 --> 00:03:52,640
And finally, notes on availability.

102
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:57,360
What you see may depend on your Microsoft 365 license,

103
00:03:57,820 --> 00:04:00,380
admin settings, and work or school environment.

104
00:04:01,040 --> 00:04:02,980
And for more advanced agents with automation

105
00:04:02,980 --> 00:04:05,280
and integrations, use Copilot Studio,

106
00:04:05,740 --> 00:04:07,300
which is only available for business

107
00:04:07,300 --> 00:04:10,800
or enterprise-level Microsoft 365 subscriptions.

